message.content:
Dependency injection in C# is basically when your class **doesn’t create its own crew**—you hand it the tools it needs from the outside, usually through the **constructor**. That makes the class easier to test, swap, and maintain because it depends on an **interface**, not some hard-coded concrete class.[1][2][6]

Think of it like this:

- Your class is the **boss**.
- Its dependencies are the **guys it needs to do the job**.
- The DI container is the **fixer** that brings the right people in at runtime.[1][5][6]

Example, gangsta-style:

```csharp
public interface IWeapon
{
    void Use();
}

public class Knife : IWeapon
{
    public void Use() => Console.WriteLine("Stab stab.");
}

public class Hitman
{
    private readonly IWeapon _weapon;

    public Hitman(IWeapon weapon)
    {
        _weapon = weapon;
    }

    public void DoWork()
    {
        _weapon.Use();
    }
}
```

Here, `Hitman` **doesn’t build** a `Knife` itself. It just says, “Bring me a weapon,” and DI hands it one.[1][2][6]

In C# and ASP.NET Core, the usual flow is:

- **Define an interface** for the dependency.
- **Implement** that interface with a concrete class.
- **Register** the mapping in the DI container.
- Let the framework **inject** it when the class is created.[1][5][6]

Why people use it:

- **Loose coupling**: classes depend on abstractions, not concrete types.[1][2][6]
- **Better testability**: you can swap real dependencies for mocks.[2][6]
- **Cleaner code**: less object-creation clutter inside your classes.[1][5]

The main way to do it is **constructor injection**; property/setter injection exists too, but constructor injection is the most common and recommended approach.[1][2][5][6]

If you want, I can also give you a **full gangster-movie analogy** for DI in ASP.NET Core, or show you how to register services in `Program.cs`.

